''''exec /usr/bin/python3 "$0" "$@" # '''
# ^ POLYGLOT INTERPRETER GUARD. `bash <this file>` would otherwise run the python source as shell
# and execute any backticked prose in it. Python reads this line as a no-op string; sh/bash
# re-execs the file under python3 before reaching any other line. mr-selfcheck enforces it.
"""mr-prune — a read-only census of the game repo's test suite: its size, the tests that are exact
twins of an earlier test in the same block, the product symbols only tests name, and signals on
test files.

    mr-prune census [--repo R] [--at REF] [--mem DIR] [--record LABEL] [--spec FILE] [--ci]
    mr-prune twin   [--repo R] [--at REF] [--mem DIR] --file F --test T --survivor S
    mr-prune keep   [--repo R] [--at REF] [--mem DIR] --file F --test T --reason TEXT
    mr-prune --selftest [ID ...]      fixtures/prune/battery.py; rc=0 pass, rc=1 fail

Defaults: R = projects/monster-realm of the checkout this tool is in; REF =
refs/remotes/origin/master; DIR = the directory this tool is in. `PRUNE-ERROR ...` on stderr and
rc=2 for a usage error, an R that is not the top level of a git repository, a REF that is not a
commit, a log that cannot be read, a --spec FILE that cannot be read, a DIR that is missing or
inside R when the command writes, or a write that fails.

A row is a candidate for someone to read, never a reason to delete a test: the deletion rule is
standards/testing-tdd.md, "Deleting a check is legitimate". No gate reads this tool's output, and
`census` exits 0 whatever it finds.

census prints, in this order:
    PRUNE-CENSUS <commit>
    totals <package> product-lines=N test-lines=N ratio=X.XX test-files=N tests=N unparsed=N ...
    totals out-of-scope evals=N sim-harness=N client-wasm=N
    duplicates <file> :: "<candidate>" -> "<survivor>"
    protected-twins <file> :: "<candidate>" -> "<survivor>" token=<token>
    orphans <file> :: "<symbol>"[ test-only] tests=["<test file>",...]
    orphan-protected <file> :: "<symbol>"[ test-only] tests=[...] token=<token>
    literal-variants <file> groups=N
    signals <file>[ source-text][ churn=N][ widened=N]
    protected-churn <file> churn=N token=<token>
    counts duplicates=N protected-twins=N identical-other-scope=N kept=N unparsed=N
    ci seconds=N conclusion=W nightly=W reruns-to-green=N           (with --ci; null when unknown)
  Packages and their test files:
    game-core       game-core/src/**/*_tests.rs, game-core/tests/*.rs
    server-module   server-module/src/**/*_tests.rs
    client          client/src/**/*.test.ts
  Product lines are the package's other .rs files outside tests/ and benches/, and client/src's
  other .ts files outside module_bindings/. A line is one newline. `tests` counts the tests the
  lexer delimits and `unparsed` the ones it cannot. The remaining keys count what the census does
  not read: inline `#[cfg(test)] mod` bodies in product files, client/e2e/**/*.spec.ts,
  client/test-util/, evals/**/*.eval.mjs, and the .rs files of sim-harness/ and client-wasm/.
  Names are JSON strings, and so is a path holding anything outside [A-Za-z0-9_./-].
  Twin rows come in file order and, within a file, definition order; the other sections are sorted
  by file, then name. `totals` and `counts` cover the whole commit.
  `--record LABEL` appends one JSON row (label, commit, totals, counts, and with --ci a `ci` object
  {seconds, conclusion, nightly, reruns_to_green}) to DIR/prune/trend.jsonl and prints
  `recorded LABEL`.
  `--spec FILE` prints only the rows with a file that a `touches:` entry of FILE names, each ending
  ` slices=<id>,...`: the `### <id> — ` slices, in file order, whose first `touches:` line under
  the heading names one. Entries are comma-separated, backticks dropped; `**` crosses directories,
  `*` and `?` do not, and an entry names everything under it as a directory. A twin row's file is
  its test file; an orphan row's files are the defining file and its tests.

Orphans. A symbol is a module-level item of game-core/src or server-module/src (top level or in a
`mod name { }` that is not `#[cfg(test)]`): `pub`/`pub(...)` fn, struct, enum, union, trait, type,
const, static; or a top-level `export` function, class, interface, type, enum or const/let/var of
client/src (module_bindings/ aside). Methods, variants and `export default` are not symbols. It is
listed when no production file but its own names it and no live code of its own file does. Live
code: an item with an attribute other than doc, inline, must_use, allow, derive, cfg_attr (an
entry point: reducer, table, wasm export, track_caller, ...; never listed), `fn main`, `const _`;
code with no name (a macro call, a top-level TypeScript statement, the initializer of a top-level
TypeScript const/let/var/type, an impl whose self type is live or is a primitive, slice or
tuple); and any item, private ones included, named by another production file or by live code of
its file. Production is every tracked text file except test files wherever they are (`*_tests.rs`,
`*.test.ts`, a `tests/` or `benches/` directory), client/test-util/, *.md and *.txt; client/e2e,
evals, client-wasm, sim-harness, HTML and config count. A file names what its identifiers and the
words inside its literals spell, comments and module-level `#[cfg(test)]` modules and items aside.
A `use ...;`, `import ...` or `export ... from ...` statement names only what it reaches through:
X of `X as Y` and of `X::...`. A file the lexer cannot read names every word of its text. An item compiled only under `#[cfg(test)]` outside a test
module is listed as `test-only`. `tests=` holds the test files whose names include the symbol. A
defining path or a symbol holding a protected token goes under `orphan-protected`. The match is by
name, so a name used for two things hides an orphan; a row is for a refactor to prove.

Signals, per test file:
  source-text   it calls include_str!, read_to_string, readFileSync or readFile (a token, not text
                in a comment or a literal)
  churn=N       N of the last 300 commits on REF changed it by 12 lines or fewer (added plus
                deleted) and left its subject file alone: same directory, stem without `_tests` or
                `.test`
  widened=N     N rows of DIR/gates/*.widened.jsonl have it as `path`
  A churned file whose path holds a protected token is also listed under `protected-churn`.
  `literal-variants` counts the groups of two or more tests of one block whose forms and bodies
  are equal once every literal is masked and that are not all the same test.

--ci reads one `gh run list --branch master` from R's directory: seconds and conclusion of the
newest completed CI run on the commit, the newest completed Nightly's conclusion, and how many of
the newest 300 CI runs concluded success on attempt 2 or later. gh missing from PATH, failing or
answering something else: the four are null, `PRUNE-WARN ci ...` goes to stderr, rc stays 0.

A test, per language:
  TypeScript  `it(title, fn)` or `test(title, fn)`, with or without `.each(table)`, written as a
              statement at the file's top level or directly in the callback of a `describe(...)` /
              `describe.sequential(...)` that is itself written so; plain string titles; a function
              literal among the arguments. Name: the describe titles and the test title joined by
              " > ". Block: the innermost describe callback.
  Rust        a `fn` with a body under an attribute whose last path segment is `test`, at the top
              level or inside `mod name { ... }` only. Name: the mod names and the fn name joined
              by "::". Block: the innermost mod.
  Anything else that declares a test is `unparsed` and appears in no row: a test built in a loop, a
  helper or a macro (`proptest!`), a computed title, any other modifier. A name taken by an earlier
  test of the file gets " #2", " #3", ... appended.
  An unlexable file has no lexed test: its lines that look like a test declaration count as
  `unparsed`, and `PRUNE-WARN unlexable <file>` goes to stderr. That is a file that is not UTF-8,
  that the lexer cannot tokenise or bracket-match, or in which the lexer finds fewer declarations
  than such lines, whatever the reason: a test line inside a block comment or a template literal,
  or text the lexer took for a string or a comment that was not one. A product .rs file the lexer
  cannot read is named the same way and adds nothing to `inline-modules`.

Twins. Two tests of one file are twins when they share the block, the form and the body, and the
body does not depend on the test's name:
  form   every token of the declaration except the name and the body: the callee and its case
         table, the callback's header, other arguments; in Rust the attributes and the
         signature.
  body   the tokens between the braces. Comments and the whitespace between tokens are not tokens;
         every literal is one token, compared as written.
  name   a test whose form or body holds one of NAME_DEPENDENT below (a snapshot matcher,
         `currentTestName`, `expect.getState`, `function_name!`, `insta::`, ...) is never a twin.
The candidate is the later test and the survivor the earliest twin in the block. The same form and
body in another block of the file is counted under `identical-other-scope`: other blocks can carry
other hooks and bindings. A pair whose path or names hold a protected token (PROTECTED below) is
listed under `protected-twins`: removing one follows the standard's protected path.
Twins can still differ when they run, through state they share: a counter one of them increments,
a table reassigned between two `.each` calls. No lexer sees that, so a row is read before anyone
acts on it, and a pair that must stay is recorded with `keep`.

twin confirms a claimed pair at a commit, T being the test to remove and S the one that stays.
F is the path as census prints it; T and S are the names themselves (a row's JSON strings,
decoded), " #2" included.
`PRUNE-TWIN-OK` (rc=0) when they are twins and T is defined after S; the line continues with
` protected=<token>` for a protected pair and ` kept` for one the keep file holds. Otherwise
`PRUNE-TWIN-FAIL <rule> ...` (rc=1). Reported alone, first that applies: `missing` (F is not a test
file at the commit), `unparsed` (F is unlexable), `missing` (T or S names no lexed test),
`same-test`. Otherwise every broken rule among `block`, `form`, `body`, `name-dependent`, `order`.
`order` is a rule because a pair confirmed in both directions would let one change remove both.

keep records a pair that is identical on purpose (the second run proves something the first cannot)
in DIR/mr-prune-keep.json as {file: {test name: {"hash", "reason"}}}. census then omits the
candidate's row and counts it under `kept` until its form or body changes. `PRUNE-KEEP-OK ...`
(rc=0) or `PRUNE-KEEP-FAIL missing | not-a-candidate | keep-file-unreadable` (rc=1, nothing
written). census and twin read a keep file they cannot parse as empty and say so on stderr, so a
broken keep file can only add rows.

The inspected repository is read through `git rev-parse`, `git ls-tree`, `git cat-file` and
`git log` at the commit; nothing is checked out and the work tree is never read. The only files written are the keep
file and the trend file, and never inside R.

Shebang is `/usr/bin/python3` on purpose: `#!/usr/bin/env python3` exits 126 in unpinned cwds under
asdf.
